# cancellation latency — how long `cancel()` takes to stop an in-flight encode
date: 2026-08-06
commit: see `git log` for eng/quality-program (before = 4c5c13246)
host: 12-core arm64 macOS (8 perf + 4 efficiency), 24 GiB, load avg 2-5 during the run
harness: rust/svtav1/examples/cancel_latency.rs
command: ./target/release/examples/cancel_latency --sizes 64,256,1024,4096 \
           --preset {13,8,6} --qp 40 --points 12 --reps 3 --baseline-reps 2 --out <tsv>
build: nice -n 19, RUSTC_WRAPPER=sccache, -j 4, release (opt-level 2), NO target-cpu=native
run:   NOT under nice — `nice -n 19` maps to background QoS on Darwin (efficiency
       cores) and distorts wall clock. The harness records the observed niceness
       in each TSV header; every row below reads `nice=0`.
metric: wall time from the instant the caller asks to stop (Instant::now()
        immediately before flipping the token) to the instant
        `try_encode_frame_420` returns — REGARDLESS of whether the encode
        honoured the cancel. Cells that returned Ok with a pending cancel are
        counted as `ignored` and their wait is still scored: an encode that runs
        another 224 ms and then returns Ok has not cancelled in 0 ms.
content: deterministic gradient (the `perf_encode`/`identity_run` recipe), 4:2:0,
         8-bit, single tile, CQP.
percentiles: nearest-rank over all scored cells at that size.

## BEFORE (4c5c13246 — the stop plumbing as it shipped)

preset 8, benchmarks/cancel_latency_p8_before_2026-08-06.tsv

  size  baseline_ms  n   honoured ignored  p50    p90     p99      max     >20ms
    64        0.394  32     23       9    0.091   0.226   0.248    0.248     0
   256        7.046  36     29       7    0.611   1.225   1.340    1.340     0
  1024       95.480  43     36       7    2.586   5.171  16.484   16.484     0
  4096      925.545  45     33      12   15.830 162.033 224.291  224.291    13

Root cause, LOCALIZED by phase instrumentation (scratch `eprintln` timers at each
`Step N` boundary of `encode_frame_impl`, 4096x4096 preset 8, 800 ms total):

  setup ->  Step 4 (MD + tile encode)      5.1 ->  535.8 ms   POLLED (per SB row)
  Step 6   (entropy walk)                535.9 ->  595.2 ms   POLLED (per SB row)
  Step 6a  (deblock level pick + apply)  595.2 ->  619.2 ms   NOT POLLED
  Step 6a' (CDEF pick + frame apply)     619.2 ->  768.1 ms   NOT POLLED  <-- coarsest
  Step 6a''(Wiener LR search + apply)    768.1 ->  768.2 ms   NOT POLLED (off at p8)
  Step 6b..8 (grain, OBU, recon, RC)     768.2 ->  777.2 ms   NOT POLLED
  return (drop of the frame's buffers)   777.2 ->  800.2 ms   n/a

The whole post-entropy tail — 205 ms, 25.6% of the frame — took no token at all.
A cancel landing anywhere in it was IGNORED: the encode ran to completion and
returned `Ok`. That is the `ignored` column above, and it is exactly the 12 cells
at f >= 0.75.

At 1024x1024 preset 6 the Wiener LR search alone is 90.4 ms of a 280 ms frame
(32%), also entirely un-polled — the same defect, a different dominant phase.

## AFTER

Polls added (all guarded by `may_stop()`, so byte-inert on the default
`Unstoppable` token — see `svtav1_encoder::stop_check`):
  - 9 phase-boundary polls in `encode_frame_impl` (Steps 4c, 5, 6a, 6a', 6a'',
    6b, 7, 7b, 8) — bounds a wait by ONE phase, not the whole tail
  - `cdef::apply_cdef_frame` / `_hbd`: per 64x64 filter-block ROW
  - `cdef::cdef_search_still` / `_hbd`: per 64x64 filter-block ROW
  - `restoration::search_restoration_still_bd`: per plane + per 256x256
    restoration unit
  - `deblock::apply_deblock_frame` / `_hbd`: per plane
  - `deblock::search_filter_level`: per step of the level bisection
  - MD search + entropy walk: per SB ROW -> per SUPERBLOCK

