VERDICT vs the 20 ms bar:
  - MET at 64x64, 256x256 and 1024x1024 at every preset measured. Worst p99
    across all of them is 4.12 ms (1024x1024 preset 6).
  - NOT MET at 4096x4096 (16.7 MP): p99 18.1 ms (p8), 21.7 ms (p6), 28.2 ms
    (p13). Down from 224 ms, and no cell is ignored any more, but 3-6 cells per
    preset still breach.
  - Zero `ignored` cells everywhere except one 64x64 cell whose 0.445 ms encode
    finished before the cancel landed (max wait 0.267 ms — immaterial).

## The 4096x4096 residual is TEARDOWN, not poll density (measured, not argued)

A 48-point fine sweep at 4096x4096 preset 8
(cancel_latency_fine_p8_2026-08-06.tsv) shows the wait is FLAT at 15-18 ms for
every cancel point from f = 0.59 to f = 0.96, and <= 5 ms below f = 0.53. A
sparse poll would produce a sawtooth (wait decreasing across each un-polled
phase); a flat floor that only appears once the frame's working set is fully
allocated is a fixed exit cost.

Corroborated independently on the SUCCESS path: in the phase profile above, the
last statement of `encode_frame_impl` (the Step 8 RC update) finishes at 777.2 ms
and the caller's timer stops at 800.2 ms — 23 ms spent dropping the frame's
buffers with no encoder work left to do.

So the remaining 4096x4096 breach is the cost of freeing a 16.7 MP frame's
working set, which the encode pays whether it is cancelled or not. Adding more
polls cannot move it; buffer reuse across frames would. Not attempted here.

## Byte-identity

Every poll is `if stop.may_stop() { stop.check()? }`. The default token is
`enough::Unstoppable`, whose `may_stop()` is `false`, so on every existing path
the poll is a not-taken branch that touches no encoder state.

Evidence: 144-cell self-fingerprint (md5 of the emitted .obu over
content={gradient,uniform} x size={64,128,256,512} x preset={2,4,6,8,10,13} x
qp={20,40,55}) is IDENTICAL before and after — re-run after each of the three
increments. Plus the workspace differential suite and the C-oracle gates; see
the commit messages.
